WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.543
Welcome.

00:00:00.543 --> 00:00:11.340
An orrery is a mechanical model of the solar system — brass, wood, and clockwork — built in the eighteenth century to show how the planets move around the Sun.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.599
This Orrery is the digital version of that idea.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:21.661
Real planetary positions, real orbital math, real missions — drawn on a screen instead of cast in metal.

00:00:21.661 --> 00:00:23.766
Look at the cards on this page.

00:00:23.766 --> 00:00:27.297
Each one takes you to a different part of the story.

00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:34.223
Explore opens the whole solar system — eight planets in their orbits, real velocities, real distances.

00:00:34.223 --> 00:00:42.846
Earth , Moon , and Mars are the places we know best — each gets its own map with every spacecraft humanity has ever sent there.

00:00:42.846 --> 00:00:50.248
ISS and Tiangong are the two space stations currently being lived in — pickable, rotatable, module by module.

00:00:50.248 --> 00:00:56.427
Missions is the catalogue of forty-two flown and planned missions, sortable and filterable.

00:00:56.427 --> 00:01:03.692
Fleet shows the hardware itself — every rocket, every rover, every spacecraft, lined up by builder and era.

00:01:03.692 --> 00:01:10.958
Science is the in-app encyclopedia — every number you see anywhere else, with the physics that produced it.

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:19.514
Plan and Fly let you design a transfer trajectory — Earth to Mars, for instance — and watch it play out in heliocentric scale.

00:01:19.514 --> 00:01:21.687
Nothing on this site is made up.

00:01:21.687 --> 00:01:26.847
Every mission has a launch date, a crew list, a destination, and a citation.

00:01:26.847 --> 00:01:30.853
Every image comes from a named agency with a named license.

00:01:30.853 --> 00:01:35.810
Every link to read further points to a primary source, not an aggregator.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.304
This isn't a textbook.

00:01:37.304 --> 00:01:44.502
It's a working model of where humans have been in space — and what we know now because someone went there.

00:01:44.502 --> 00:01:45.452
Take any card.

00:01:45.452 --> 00:01:46.403
Take the tour.

00:01:46.403 --> 00:01:54.959
When you find something that doesn't make sense, the tiny question-mark chips open the Science encyclopedia at the right page.

00:01:54.959 --> 00:02:02.971
When you find something beautiful, look for the share button — every view in Orrery has a URL you can send to someone.

00:02:02.971 --> 00:02:04.465
Welcome to the orrery.

00:02:04.465 --> 00:02:05.280
Pick a card.

